Anonymous enums are useful when you only need the enumerator constants and not a named type. They are frequently used for bit flags:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

enum { // Anonymous enum for bit flags
    READ_PERMISSION  = 1 << 0, // 0001
    WRITE_PERMISSION = 1 << 1, // 0010
    EXEC_PERMISSION  = 1 << 2  // 0100
};

int main() {
    int user_permissions = READ_PERMISSION | EXEC_PERMISSION;
    if (user_permissions & READ_PERMISSION) {
        printf("User has read permission.\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```
An anonymous `enum` is suitable because we don't need to declare variables of type `enum <tag>`. We only need the symbolic integer constants (`READ_PERMISSION`, etc.) which are directly available in the current scope.
